        "title": "Apply"
      },
      "description": "The one write of the decision graph: a batch of ops, applied in order and committed together, or refused whole with nothing written. Each op is checked against the graph the earlier ops left, so add a component before its decisions and a decision before those that depend on it. A refusal names the first op at fault (`op`), the argument (`field`) and the `rule`.\nOps (`op` tag): record a decision (always `proposed`; carries `origin`); revise any of a decision's fields, a list whole or by `add_*`/`remove_*` (never both for one list; a removed element must be held); rename (rewrites every edge to it); merge `absorbed` into `survivor` (edges repointed, anchors, tags and edges unioned, the absorbed text dropped); remove (refused while decisions depend on it, unless `repoint_to` names their new target); add_component, revise_component, rename_component (carries its decisions and connections), remove_component (refused while in use).\nA revise that changes nothing, an edge to a missing node or to itself, and an edge that closes a depends_on cycle are refused. Revising the choice, reason or alternatives of an accepted decision proposes the new text and keeps the accepted one; revising back to it accepts it again. Anchors, tags, edges and renames leave the status alone.\nQuality: each field a record, revise or merge changes meets the bar. Built in: a one-line choice of at most 200 bytes, a reason of real text, at least one alternative and one anchor; then the limits and patterns of `[quality]` in project.toml. A refusal names the field, the rule, the measured value and the limit; a field the op leaves as it is is not judged. A `warn` pattern writes the batch and lists its match in `warnings`.\nThe result names each op's node and what it changed, never the texts.",
